Emergency Contraception Consultation
General Practice Other
Unprotected intercourse within 72-120 hours, Assessment of menstrual cycle timing, Administration of levonorgestrel/ulipristal acetate, Provision of ongoing contraceptive advice

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-015
Reason for Visit: Extreme anxiety and panic requiring urgent medical intervention following a condom failure during intercourse occurring near the time of suspected ovulation.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage noted a highly distressed patient who experienced a barrier method failure approximately 12 hours ago. The patient is tracking their cycle and believes they are currently ovulating, leading to severe acute anxiety regarding an unintended pregnancy. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to assess the precise timing of the cycle, determine the most highly effective emergency contraceptive agent (ulipristal acetate vs levonorgestrel), and administer the medication as rapidly as possible to maximize efficacy before ovulation occurs. Delaying assessment until the next in-hours period significantly increased the risk of the emergency contraception failing.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "The condom broke last night, my tracking app says I'm ovulating today, and I am absolutely terrified I'm going to get pregnant."
History of Present Illness: The patient presents with a recent history of suspected barrier method failure occurring approximately 12 hours prior to the consultation. The patient exhibits extreme anxiety and concern regarding the potential for an unintended pregnancy. A detailed assessment of the specific timing of the unprotected intercourse relative to the patient's menstrual cycle reveals the patient is currently on day 14 of a highly regular 28-day cycle, indicating a high-risk window for fertilization. The patient denies any nausea, vomiting, or pelvic pain. They have never used emergency contraception previously.
Functional Status: Independent, fully functional, but currently exhibiting high emotional distress.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe acute situational anxiety; patient is adamant about avoiding pregnancy due to strict personal and occupational reasons.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 125/80 | HR: 95 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 18 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 64 Kg
General: Anxious, hyper-fixated on the menstrual tracking app on their phone, rapidly asking questions about efficacy.
Physical Exam: Abdominal: Soft, non-tender, no palpable masses or guarding. The clinical encounter mandates a discussion regarding the differing efficacies of levonorgestrel versus ulipristal acetate based on the elapsed time and current cycle phase. Expected investigation results include a negative point-of-care urine pregnancy test 3 to 4 weeks post-exposure ensuring the emergency contraception was successful.
Environmental Observations: Safe, private home environment allowing for confidential reproductive health discussions.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications; denies any use of liver-enzyme inducing medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Emergency Contraception Consultation (High-Risk Ovulatory Window) - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Established Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Patient had a confirmed, normal menstrual period 14 days ago, and a point-of-care urine beta-hCG test performed during the visit is negative.

Pelvic Inflammatory Disease - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of pelvic pain, fever, or abnormal vaginal discharge.

Ectopic Pregnancy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Negative pregnancy test and absence of any abdominal or pelvic pain.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient experienced a high-risk barrier method failure precisely during their calculated ovulatory window, presenting with acute situational panic. Telephone triage correctly identified the time-critical nature of emergency contraception. Urgent face-to-face assessment was mandated to perform a cycle assessment and provide the most effective pharmacological intervention immediately. Because the patient is likely surging luteinizing hormone (LH) today, Levonorgestrel may be ineffective. Ulipristal acetate was clinically indicated as it can delay ovulation even after the LH surge has begun. Deferring this intervention until the next in-hours period would have allowed ovulation to occur, rendering any subsequent emergency contraception completely useless and resulting in an unacceptable risk of unintended pregnancy.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Conducted a thorough cycle analysis. Administered oral Ulipristal Acetate 30mg stat (directly observed). Provided a comprehensive explanation of the mechanism of action primarily delaying or inhibiting ovulation. Advised regarding the potential for an altered or delayed upcoming menstrual period, and provided counselling regarding the absolute lack of protection against sexually transmitted infections.
Positive Behavioral Change: To secure reliable, long-term pregnancy prevention and remove this anxiety entirely, commit to booking a consultation for a Long-Acting Reversible Contraceptive (like an IUD or implant) tomorrow morning. Write the clinic's phone number on a sticky note and place it directly on your bathroom mirror tonight. This highly visible visual cue forces you to confront the task first thing in the morning, translating tonight's acute anxiety into a proactive, permanent health solution.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Mandatory GP review in 3 to 4 weeks to perform a follow-up urine pregnancy test and finalize ongoing contraceptive plans.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Seek immediate emergency medical care via 000 or the nearest Emergency Department if you develop sudden, severe, doubling-over pelvic pain, heavy vaginal bleeding, or if you feel faint and dizzy.

